WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.920
- You're going to learn the three steps

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:02.940
to help you tap into

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:06.000
the power of reflective listening skills.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.940
And after the tips, you'll have
the opportunity to practice

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:10.530
in real time with me.

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:12.900
So you'll leave this video
with some repetitions

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.340
and I'll give you some pro tips

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:18.000
at the very end to help you
improve on your own from there.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.860
Be sure to look in the description below

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:23.280
for a list of all the other
videos on listening that I have

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.470
and a link to a free download on the Top 5

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:28.500
Essential Communication
Skills for Professionals.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.960
A basic definition of reflective
listening is to listen

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.420
so you understand what
the person is saying

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:34.650
and then reflect

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.530
or show the speaker that
you have understood them.

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:40.470
And this is why reflective
listening is so powerful.

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:44.040
You are helping people
feel heard and understood.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:45.660
I mean, what price would you put

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:47.790
on that if somebody was really hearing you

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:49.500
and really understanding you?

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:51.000
Your reflective listening will also

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:53.640
help the other person
get their own clarity.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.490
That speaker has the opportunity
to hear themselves talk

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:57.330
through an issue.

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:00.000
Sometimes all they need
is a sounding board

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.210
and listening like this
builds trust as well.

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:06.540
Let's talk about what
reflective listening is not.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:10.650
It's not listening to
offer solutions or advice.

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:13.050
Obviously in the course of a relationship

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:15.900
it's possible that advice
may come out at some point

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.360
maybe even near the end
of the same conversation.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:20.100
But giving advice is not part

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.680
of reflective listening specifically.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:24.840
So if you're a fixer like I am

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:28.140
sometimes reflective listening
may be a challenge for you.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:29.730
You have to resist the temptation

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:33.000
to short circuit the
listening process by jumping

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.020
in with advice before the
other person truly feels heard.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:38.730
Counselors and therapists

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:40.530
use reflective listening constantly.

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:44.340
I'm not a therapist, but
reflective listening can be used

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.200
in your personal or family relationships.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.430
If you're a coach, teacher, a
supervisor, or have coworkers

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:52.680
you can use elements
of reflective listening

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:54.330
in lots of situations.

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:56.790
In fact, you probably
already have moments where

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:58.770
you make reflective statements.

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:02.010
If your wife tells you, I
finally mailed that package

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:04.920
to my sister, you might reply, you did it.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.100
If your best friend says,
this project is such a weight

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:11.550
on my shoulders, you might
reply, it's a heavy load.

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:13.740
Those are little reflective statements

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:15.750
and reflective listening just dives deeper

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:17.400
into those waters.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:18.720
So here are three steps.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.320
Then we'll practice, and I'll
finish with some pro tips.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.840
First, your top priority
will be to listen carefully

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:26.700
to understand the other person.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:28.650
We'll usually be listening
for this struggle,

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.650
but we could also be listening
for good news as well.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:34.830
But just realizing in the
first place that the situation

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:38.160
calls for some reflective
listening is part of this.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.590
Unless you're a counselor,
when a person walks in the room

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:43.110
and starts talking, it's
not always clear what

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:44.490
the other person needs from you.

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:48.000
So as they talk, listen to
understand the big picture.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.577
You might be able to tell that
something's bothering them

00:02:50.577 --> 00:02:52.290
and they may be generally upset.

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:55.380
For example, you can sense
that they have a challenge

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:57.000
or difficulty that they're going through.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.040
That's usually a first indicator

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.740
that you should go into
reflective listening mode.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:03.720
This is where you say to yourself, okay

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:05.310
something's bothering them.

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:06.960
Now let's do some reflective listening

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:08.580
and see where it goes.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:09.720
So I'm starting with this point

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:13.110
because for the average
listener, it's not always obvious

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.870
that a conversation is going
to go in this direction.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:18.870
You might normally be
in problem solving mode.

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:21.900
Either way, once you realize
the conversation calls

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.360
for reflective listening, then listen

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:25.920
for anything that gives you clues

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:27.540
about this specific emotion

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:30.600
or cognitive struggle
they are experiencing.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.030
Second, make a reflective statement.

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:34.890
What exactly is bothering them?

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:37.980
Your goal is to understand
their emotions at the same level

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:40.740
of sophistication that
they're expressing them.

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:43.860
If all you can express is that
they're feeling sad or down

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:46.140
then start there and reflect that.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:47.940
But if you can tell specifically

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:50.490
that they are anxious, insecure, or hurt,

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:52.110
then you'd reflect back to them

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:54.570
that level of detail and nuance.

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:57.120
Maybe they're showing
signs of being conflicted,

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:59.670
having mixed or layered emotions.

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:01.740
Whatever it is, you
take what you've noticed

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:04.890
and make a short statement
that reflects back

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:06.210
what you're hearing.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:07.830
The goal of your statement is to show

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:09.207
that you are trying to understand them

00:04:09.207 --> 00:04:11.880
and it also gives them the opportunity to

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:13.770
express themselves a little further.

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:15.600
Here are some starter samples

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:18.360
and will practice these
together in a couple of minutes.

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:21.390
I'm going to fill in the
emotions in these samples

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:24.330
but these samples work
virtually for any struggle.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.910
It sounds like you're
feeling disappointed,

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:31.050
you're feeling helpless about
it, you felt embarrassed.

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:32.730
Those reflective statements are all

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:34.500
about different emotional words.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:37.500
The speaker likely already said
that you're reflecting back

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:40.080
to them, but some people
are more likely to tell you

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.540
about how they think versus how they feel.

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:45.090
For example, they might
be struggling cognitively

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:46.230
about a big decision

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:48.870
or trying to understand
what's really going

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:50.430
on in a situation.

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:52.440
So here's what a reflective
statement sounds like.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:55.740
When the person is talking
about what's on their mind

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:58.500
you sound like you are
weighing your options.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:01.020
You're not sure what to make of all of it.

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:03.960
It sounds like you're not
ready to pull the trigger.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:04.920
Whether you're talking

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:07.230
about their emotions or their thoughts,

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:09.390
let's point to some quick commonalities.

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:11.850
First, these are all statements
which we'll explain more

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:12.750
in a minute.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:14.970
They're also concise and that's important

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:17.580
because when you're listening reflectively

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.430
the other person is the one
talking almost the entire time.

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:22.710
So keep your part concise.

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:25.352
Here are some additional
sample starter sentences.

00:05:25.352 --> 00:05:30.150
You're wondering if,
for you it feels as if.

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:31.860
And then you would of
course fill in the rest

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:35.160
of that statement with whatever
you're hearing from them.

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:36.960
Common question I hear is, well

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:38.670
what about asking them questions?

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:40.740
Well, yes, you can ask questions

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:42.410
to clarify your own understanding

00:05:42.410 --> 00:05:44.370
in the course of the conversation.

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:47.550
That's part of effective
listening generally.

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:49.230
If asking a question helps you

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:50.850
understand something better than ask,

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:53.370
you can also make reflective statements

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:55.950
and add a small tag sentence.

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:57.360
Here are some samples.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:59.970
Let's assume you just made
your reflective statement

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:01.640
and you can add a brief tag.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:03.360
Am I tracking with you?

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:04.800
Is that accurate?

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:07.470
Am I understanding you clearly?

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:09.180
Tag questions can be a helpful way to

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:11.580
invite the other person to clarify

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:14.460
or tell more of their
story, but here's the key.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:16.890
The part of your statement
that mirrors their

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:19.920
struggle is what makes
this reflective listening.

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:22.440
The act of asking a question is not

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:24.450
what makes it reflective listening.

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:26.760
Reflective listening
is all about restating

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:29.880
and clarifying what the other
person has already said.

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:33.570
In fact, if this were a video
specifically for counselors

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:36.420
we would probably advise
you not to ask a lot

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:38.400
of full-blown questions when you are

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:40.470
in reflective listening mode.

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:42.990
But quick tag questions may be helpful

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:44.460
if it fits your style.

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:45.510
So here's the heart of it.

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:46.530
You can see

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:50.010
in the few samples we've looked
at so far, you're a mirror.

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:52.140
You're not telling them what you believe

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:53.730
you're not speculating

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:55.890
about what you think
they're really feeling.

00:06:55.890 --> 00:06:58.470
You're not directing or
steering the conversation

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:01.080
to where you think they
should go or trying to

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:04.020
provide your own label or
reframe their experience.

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:05.550
You're not trying to change their mind

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:07.860
or help them see things differently.

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:10.470
I say this because I know
a few people who almost

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:13.500
immediately try to reframe
or spin anything I say

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:16.560
to them so then they know
how to deal with it better.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:18.060
That's not reflective listening.

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:21.120
Remember what I said earlier,
you're not trying to fix them.

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:25.380
A good reflective statement
simply restates or offers back

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:27.870
exactly what you believe
the other person is saying.

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:29.910
The basic impression that you're getting

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:31.620
there's a neutrality to it.

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:34.860
It's concise, reflective
listening isn't easy

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:36.600
but when you see it in action

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:40.020
it's much more straightforward
than most people realize.

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:43.200
And third, after you have
offered a reflective statement

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:45.960
you go right back into pure listening mode

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:47.730
and let them respond.

00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:50.820
You zip it and let them
talk in other words,

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:52.830
and this is really simply going back

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:54.480
to step one, but I'm calling it step three

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:57.000
because this is a process,
and when you see yourself

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:59.910
going back into pure
listening mode that completes

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:02.910
the circle, the other
person is now responding

00:08:02.910 --> 00:08:05.940
to what you have reflected, your feedback.

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:07.950
Step three is not offering advice.

00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:10.410
In other words, you're
listening and giving them

00:08:10.410 --> 00:08:14.370
the opportunity to process and
respond to your reflection.

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:17.310
The speaker will usually
react in one of two ways

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:18.960
or sometimes in between.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:21.840
They'll either show agreement,
yeah, I'm incredibly

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:25.230
frustrated about it, or
maybe they'll clarify, well

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:27.120
I wouldn't exactly say frustrated.

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:29.460
I'm just annoyed that it's taking so long.

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:31.710
And then the conversation
would continue and you

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:33.270
would continue listening.

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:34.860
And even if you're a little off

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:37.080
in your reflective statement,
you're still helping them

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:40.410
explore and increase
their self-understanding.

00:08:40.410 --> 00:08:43.050
When you pitch back
what you heard them say

00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:45.510
that gives them the
opportunity to look at what

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:48.030
they're experiencing at arm's length.

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:51.000
They might even use the
word anxious, but when they

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:54.510
hear you reflect that
word anxious back to them,

00:08:54.510 --> 00:08:56.820
they have the opportunity
to ask themselves

00:08:56.820 --> 00:08:58.830
is that really what I'm feeling?

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:00.390
That reflective process leads

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:02.670
to more clarity for the speaker.

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:04.350
As the conversation moves forward

00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:07.050
you just go back into step number one

00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:09.750
and repeat that a few
times in the conversation.

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:11.580
You listen carefully to understand

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:14.070
and get an impression of
what the person's feeling.

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:16.560
You make a reflective
statement and then you go back

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:19.200
into pure listening mode as they respond.

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:20.880
Let's practice this together.

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:23.220
I'll summarize the
situation I'm dealing with

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:25.410
and I'll put some sample
reflective statements

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:28.650
up and you'll practice your
responses out loud with me.

00:09:28.650 --> 00:09:31.860
Again, listen for the feelings
or the mental struggles

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:34.770
your reflecting back what you hear.

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:36.300
We'll do three quick scenarios

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:38.430
from easiest to most difficult.

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:39.840
Scenario one is the easiest.

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:43.290
Let's say I come to you to talk
about why I've been so busy

00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:47.310
for the last six months and
how it's taking a toll on me.

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:48.900
And somewhere in my story I say

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:51.630
I feel exhausted and overwhelmed.

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:54.420
In your mind, what am I feeling
when I tell you this story?

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:58.140
Identify my emotion and pause
the video if you need to.

00:09:58.140 --> 00:10:00.180
Now you'll reflect back what you've heard.

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:02.610
Say this out loud with
me and fill in the blank

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:06.720
with whatever emotion you
sensed I was experiencing.

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:11.720
It sounds like you are
feeling you are about that.

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:15.930
You feel if you said anything close

00:10:15.930 --> 00:10:18.840
to exhausted or overwhelmed,
then you're right on track.

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:21.000
And that was designed to be the easiest

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:24.450
because I literally said I
was exhausted and overwhelmed.

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:28.020
You may have had to adjust
your sentences a little bit to

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:30.660
match what I was saying, but
if you said anything close

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:34.320
to exhausted or overwhelmed,
then you're right on track.

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:37.260
That was designed to be the
easiest because I literally said

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:39.450
that I was exhausted and overwhelmed.

00:10:39.450 --> 00:10:41.970
But again, even if you used another word

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:44.640
that would give me the
opportunity to clarify

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:46.770
and keep talking from there, and even

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:49.200
though these statements
sound really straightforward

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:50.700
if you were the person speaking

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:52.770
and somebody did reflective listening

00:10:52.770 --> 00:10:55.110
you would feel heard and understood.

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:57.510
So it doesn't sound
like you're saying much

00:10:57.510 --> 00:11:00.660
but it absolutely makes
a difference to them.

00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:02.700
Scenario two is a little
bit more difficult.

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:06.630
In this scenario, I won't
label my struggles as clearly

00:11:06.630 --> 00:11:08.580
and I'll give you what I'm
feeling and you come up with

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:10.770
an appropriate name and reflect that back.

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:12.360
So here's the scenario.

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:14.790
I've come to talk about
an important decision

00:11:14.790 --> 00:11:17.280
I need to make that's tearing me up.

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:21.600
I don't know if I should
take job A or job B.

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:24.210
In your mind, what do
you believe I am feeling?

00:11:24.210 --> 00:11:28.050
Identify my struggle and
pause the video if it helps.

00:11:28.050 --> 00:11:30.210
Now reflect back what you heard.

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:32.820
Say these statements allowed
with me and fill in the blank

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:35.613
with whatever emotion you
believe I'm experiencing.

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:38.133
It sounds like you are feeling.

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:42.120
You are about that.

00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:43.830
You feel.

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:44.663
If you said anything

00:11:44.663 --> 00:11:48.300
like it sounds like you are
torn, conflicted, or ambivalent

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:49.620
then you're on the right track.

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:51.840
I didn't tell you all the pros

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:55.020
and the cons of job A versus job B

00:11:55.020 --> 00:11:58.230
but you could tell that I
didn't know which one to choose.

00:11:58.230 --> 00:12:00.180
Scenario three will be
the most challenging.

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:01.830
And here's a hint for you.

00:12:01.830 --> 00:12:04.320
Sometimes people feel
more than one emotion.

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:06.990
Maybe there's something
positive and something negative

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:08.940
and that's what's happening
here in this scenario.

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:10.680
So here it is, I'm moving

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:13.605
to a new location a few
hours away to take advantage

00:12:13.605 --> 00:12:16.200
of a professional opportunity.

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:18.870
On the positive side,
it could be huge for me.

00:12:18.870 --> 00:12:20.820
I think I'll excel in this role

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:22.650
and that gets me fired up.

00:12:22.650 --> 00:12:23.790
On the negative side,

00:12:23.790 --> 00:12:25.980
I don't know anybody in this new community

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:28.500
and I'll be far from all
my friends and family.

00:12:28.500 --> 00:12:31.530
I'm not sure if I'll
be able to handle that.

00:12:31.530 --> 00:12:34.410
So in your mind, what do
you believe I'm feeling?

00:12:34.410 --> 00:12:36.660
Identify both sides of my struggle.

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:38.730
And pause the video if it helps.

00:12:38.730 --> 00:12:40.920
Now reflect back what you heard.

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:42.930
We're only going to do
this with one statement

00:12:42.930 --> 00:12:47.130
so do your best to fill in the
two blanks allowed with me.

00:12:47.130 --> 00:12:49.710
It sounds like you are feeling both.

00:12:49.710 --> 00:12:52.740
And now the good news is.

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:55.350
There are multiple right
ways to answer this.

00:12:55.350 --> 00:12:58.680
Here are a few good ways to
reflect back what you've heard.

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:00.930
It sounds like you're
feeling both positives

00:13:00.930 --> 00:13:03.240
and negatives about this move.

00:13:03.240 --> 00:13:04.260
It sounds like you're feeling

00:13:04.260 --> 00:13:08.190
both excited and nervous about this move.

00:13:08.190 --> 00:13:11.070
And even if you simply
said you feel conflicted

00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:12.690
or you feel mixed emotions

00:13:12.690 --> 00:13:16.380
those are still pretty good
reflective listening statements.

00:13:16.380 --> 00:13:17.610
And feel free to rewind

00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:20.250
and practice the three scenarios again

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:23.220
because simple starter
sentences can be used

00:13:23.220 --> 00:13:26.700
for almost any situation and
it'll help you get repetitions.

00:13:26.700 --> 00:13:29.220
Here are some pro tips to
help you get better at this

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:30.720
as you move forward.

00:13:30.720 --> 00:13:32.250
First, reflect back

00:13:32.250 --> 00:13:35.760
at the same level of
nuance they are expressing.

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:38.820
Reflective listening can
feel so simplistic at first

00:13:38.820 --> 00:13:40.500
that you may feel stupid

00:13:40.500 --> 00:13:42.120
saying these reflective statements.

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:43.950
To you, it might seem really obvious

00:13:43.950 --> 00:13:45.930
to say you're feeling anxious

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:48.210
or it sounds like you're
feeling depressed.

00:13:48.210 --> 00:13:49.500
And on the one hand, that might be true.

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:51.780
If the person is talking
about their struggle

00:13:51.780 --> 00:13:54.840
for five minutes and you
oversimplify it too much

00:13:54.840 --> 00:13:59.160
by saying you feel bad, then
yes, Einstein, I feel bad.

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:00.240
That's so obvious.

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:02.100
It's probably not helpful.

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:04.500
To avoid that overly simplistic feeling,

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:07.440
do your best to understand
and reflect back to them

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:09.720
at the same level of
sophistication that they are

00:14:09.720 --> 00:14:11.070
showing you.

00:14:11.070 --> 00:14:14.220
That might mean you reflect
back the various reasons

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:16.950
for how they're feeling
or reflect back the most

00:14:16.950 --> 00:14:20.040
pivotal details of what they've just said.

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:22.140
Just be sure to be concise and not take

00:14:22.140 --> 00:14:25.140
over the conversation
and that takes practice.

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:27.120
Now, on the other hand,
you might be surprised

00:14:27.120 --> 00:14:30.150
at how helpful reflecting
a basic emotion can be

00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:31.380
for some people.

00:14:31.380 --> 00:14:33.390
I was talking to somebody
the other day who

00:14:33.390 --> 00:14:36.437
to me seemed obviously
depressed, but they never

00:14:36.437 --> 00:14:38.460
realized it themselves.

00:14:38.460 --> 00:14:41.280
They told me that their therapist
had said to them, it seems

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:42.777
like you're feeling depressed.

00:14:42.777 --> 00:14:46.470
And that was a very helpful
reflective moment for them

00:14:46.470 --> 00:14:47.910
in their therapy session.

00:14:47.910 --> 00:14:50.460
And that leads to pro tip number two,

00:14:50.460 --> 00:14:55.460
build your vocabulary for
emotional and cognitive dilemmas.

00:14:55.500 --> 00:14:58.770
For example, you may want
to Google emotion wheel

00:14:58.770 --> 00:15:00.600
and you'll see a circular diagram

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:03.000
with basic emotions on the inside

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:07.170
of the wheel and increasingly
differentiated emotions

00:15:07.170 --> 00:15:08.400
each layer moving outwards.

00:15:08.400 --> 00:15:10.830
So sad would be a basic emotion

00:15:10.830 --> 00:15:14.010
but loneliness is a more
specific type of sadness.

00:15:14.010 --> 00:15:15.900
Scared is a basic emotion

00:15:15.900 --> 00:15:20.040
but anxious or embarrassed
or more specific than scared.

00:15:20.040 --> 00:15:23.160
So as you improve your
vocabulary of emotions

00:15:23.160 --> 00:15:25.440
you'll be able to
reflect back the emotions

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:28.470
that people are experiencing
with more precision.

00:15:28.470 --> 00:15:30.480
That adds clarity and adds value

00:15:30.480 --> 00:15:33.510
because the other person
will feel truly heard

00:15:33.510 --> 00:15:34.980
and understood.

00:15:34.980 --> 00:15:38.400
And third, reflective
listening is truly in art.

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:39.810
These are fundamental skills

00:15:39.810 --> 00:15:42.000
but you will get better
and better at those skills

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:43.440
the more you do them.

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:46.200
You might feel a bit like a robot at first

00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:48.690
but practicing will make
it feel more natural.

00:15:48.690 --> 00:15:50.190
Your timing will be better.

00:15:50.190 --> 00:15:52.200
Your non-verbal cues will be better.

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:54.720
Those are good components
of active listening

00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:57.270
and all the related skills
that you'll get better

00:15:57.270 --> 00:15:58.830
at with practice.

00:15:58.830 --> 00:16:00.630
You may feel more relaxed in time

00:16:00.630 --> 00:16:03.120
and that'll help the
speaker relax as well.

00:16:03.120 --> 00:16:03.953
So go out there

00:16:03.953 --> 00:16:05.820
and start practicing
reflective listening so

00:16:05.820 --> 00:16:07.050
you get better at it.

00:16:07.050 --> 00:16:09.150
Be sure to download that
free PDF I mentioned

00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:11.940
about the five essential
professional communication skills

00:16:11.940 --> 00:16:13.800
that all professionals should have

00:16:13.800 --> 00:16:16.620
and a list of other listening
skills that I've made.

00:16:16.620 --> 00:16:17.760
Until next time, thanks.

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:19.210
God bless and I will see you.

